<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net8.0;net9.0;net10.0;net11.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AssemblyName>Com.H</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Com.H</RootNamespace>
    <Version>11.0.1</Version>
    <Authors>Hussein Al Bayati</Authors>
    <Description>General purpose library containing, in its current form, a very small collection of helpful functionalties that don't warrent a seperate library for each at this early stage.</Description>
    <Copyright>Hussein Al Bayati</Copyright>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/H7O/Com.H</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageIcon>128x128.jpg</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>nuget.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>general purpose</PackageTags>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>
11.0.1

Fixes in three methods of Com.H.Text that read dates and numbers out of a text. No API was added,
changed or removed.

ExtractInts and ExtractRangeInts, which read whole numbers:

* Fixed: a number written in the digits of another script, such as Arabic-Indic, Persian or
  fullwidth digits, threw a FormatException. The methods found such digits and could not read
  them. They are now read as the numbers they are.
* ExtractRangeInts takes the Arabic comma, the fullwidth comma and the ideographic comma between
  two entries of a list, beside the ordinary comma. They are the commas typed with those digits,
  and any other character between two numbers makes a range of them.

ExtractDates, which reads dates:

* Fixed: an entry that is not a date threw a FormatException if it held a word, a space and the
  letter d, as "next day" does, or the letter d on both sides of a hyphen, as "mid-december" does.
  It is now left out, like every other entry that is not a date. Two patterns meant for dates
  without a year looked for the letter d where they meant a digit.
* Fixed: a month's name and a day without a year, such as "Nov 5", is that day of the current year
  in every culture, as the method's description says. It was so only where the culture writes the
  month first, as en-US and the invariant culture do. Where the culture writes the day first, as
  en-GB, de-DE and ar-AE do, the number was taken for a year, which gave November 1 of 2005. The
  name can be in English or in the culture's own language.
* Changed, in those day-first cultures only: a month's name with a number that is not a day of
  that month, such as "Feb 30" or "Mar 45", is left out. It gave February 1 of 2030 and March 1
  of 1945.
* Not changed: where the month is written first, every month's name with every number gives what
  it gave before. And a date in numbers without a year, such as "11-05", is still read in the
  culture's own order: November 5 where the month comes first, May 11 where the day does.

Upgrading from before 11.0.0? 11.0.0 added the net11.0 build and changed no API. 10.3.4 changed
the type GetDataModelParameters gives a JSON integer from 2^53 on, 10.3.3 how Fill and FillDate
treat a value that holds a placeholder, 10.3.1 what IOExtensions.UnifyPathSeperator does with a
leading // or \\ on Windows, and 10.3.0 how Com.H.Shell and Com.H.Pdf behave. The notes of 10.3.0
to 11.0.0 list every change:
https://www.nuget.org/packages/Com.H/11.0.0#releasenotes-body-tab
https://www.nuget.org/packages/Com.H/10.3.4#releasenotes-body-tab
https://www.nuget.org/packages/Com.H/10.3.3#releasenotes-body-tab
https://www.nuget.org/packages/Com.H/10.3.2#releasenotes-body-tab
https://www.nuget.org/packages/Com.H/10.3.1#releasenotes-body-tab
https://www.nuget.org/packages/Com.H/10.3.0#releasenotes-body-tab
    </PackageReleaseNotes>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/H7O/Com.H</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>True</PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    
    <!-- Deterministic builds for reproducibility. ContinuousIntegrationBuild rewrites source
         paths to "/_/" so the package carries no local folder names. It is on for every Release
         build, not only on CI, so a pack from Visual Studio's right-click Pack comes out the
         same as one from the command line or GitHub Actions. -->
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true' or '$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true' or '$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    
    <!-- Source Link - enables stepping into source code when debugging -->
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    
    <!-- Embed debug symbols in the assembly (avoids separate .snupkg) -->
    <DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

    <!-- Put the source itself in the PDB, not just a Source Link URL pointing at it, so that
         stepping does not depend on the compiler's source-path rewrite landing (a
         case-sensitive prefix match that parallel inner builds can silently miss) or on
         GitHub being reachable. See SOURCELINK-STEPPING.md. -->
    <EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>

    <!-- Stop a pack before it builds anything unless it is Release. See RequireReleaseForPack. -->
    <BeforePack>RequireReleaseForPack;$(BeforePack)</BeforePack>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Visual Studio's Pack command packs whichever configuration is selected, and a Debug
       package is unoptimised and compiled with DEBUG defined. -->
  <Target Name="RequireReleaseForPack" Condition="'$(Configuration)' != 'Release'">
    <Error Text="Com.H must be packed from the Release configuration, but this pack is $(Configuration). Switch the configuration to Release, or run: dotnet pack .\src\Com.H.csproj -c Release" />
  </Target>

  <!-- Source Link for GitHub -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub" Version="10.0.402" PrivateAssets="All" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Polyfill packages that bring netstandard2.0 to feature parity with the
       modern targets (dynamic runtime binder, System.Text.Json, IAsyncEnumerable).
       Versions are kept in lockstep with Com.H.Data.Common so consumers referencing
       both packages don't hit conflicting transitive versions. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CSharp" Version="4.7.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Text.Json" Version="11.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces" Version="11.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\128x128.jpg">
      <Pack>True</Pack>
      <PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
    </None>
    <None Include="..\nuget.md">
      <Pack>True</Pack>
      <PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
